#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173140 :tutorial/content "We know that the absolute value operation does nothing to a number if it is non-negative. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|0| & =0 \\\\ |1| & =1 \\\\ |2| & =2 \\\\ |3| & =3 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this fact as follows:\n\n$$\n|x| = x \\quad \\text{ if } \\quad x \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe also know that $x^2$ is non-negative for any value of $x$. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\left|x^2\\right| = x^2\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is *any* real number." :tutorial/id #uuid "b20a7fe3-4627-5ed7-a7dd-afd383fa3c59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1716 :tutorial/title "The Absolute Value of a Square"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173141 :tutorial/content "We know that $|x|=x$ if $x \\geq 0.$ But what if $x < 0?$\n\nIf a variable is known to be negative, then the absolute value changes it to positive. To turn a negative number into a positive number, we multiply by $-1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n|x|  = -x \\quad\\textrm{for}\\quad x < 0.\n$$\n\nTo convince ourselves that this is indeed true, let's verify it in the case when $x=-2.$ Substituting this value into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-2| & =-(-2)=2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "93c4f77c-611c-5870-88b3-e76b8bbc7275" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1831 :tutorial/title "The Absolute Value of a Negative Variable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173210 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of absolute value. \n\nLet's first remind ourselves of the rules of absolute value:\n\n- $|{-a}| = |a|$\n\n- $|a \\cdot b| = |a| \\cdot |b|$\n\n- $\\left| \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right| = \\dfrac{|a|}{|b|}$\n\nRecall that the last two rules are known as the **distributive properties.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d23d2e9-6bc0-5a8b-bdb3-84a2f0e9d4d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6010 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
